<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">How to read publication status</p>
    <h2>Accepted is meaningful, but it is not the final typeset article</h2>
    <p>Frontiers currently identifies the hydration review as accepted and assigns a DOI. The publisher also states that the final formatted version will be published soon. That means the scholarly work has passed the journal's acceptance stage, while copyediting, typesetting or final publication processing may still be underway.</p>
    <p>When that status changes, this page should be updated to the final citation rather than leaving stale language such as “accepted” indefinitely.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Living record</span>
    <h3>Publication metadata should change when the publisher record changes.</h3>
    <p>We prefer a smaller verified list over a larger list padded with unverified projects, drafts or marketing summaries.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Evidence types</p>
  <h2>Not every research output answers the same question</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Review article</h3><p>Synthesizes prior literature and can identify mechanisms, gaps and research priorities. It does not directly test the finished product unless the reviewed evidence includes such studies.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Mechanistic study</h3><p>Tests biological or physicochemical processes. Useful for explaining why a formulation may work, but translation to meaningful outcomes still requires further evidence.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Product study</h3><p>Tests the actual formulation, dose and preparation under a defined protocol. This is the most direct route to product-specific evidence.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Implementation study</h3><p>Asks whether a product or hydration approach works acceptably in a real setting, population or delivery system beyond controlled laboratory conditions.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Citation principle</p>
    <h2>Use the strongest source that actually supports the sentence</h2>
    <p>A product page should not cite a broad review to imply a product effect that was never measured. A physiology page may appropriately cite a mechanistic review. A clinical claim should be supported by evidence in the relevant population and by the product's authorized conditions of use where applicable.</p>
    <p>This principle keeps scientific education, product evidence and regulated claims connected without collapsing them into one category.</p>
  </div>
</section>

